1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,500
Arjun feels ungrateful asking for room to think.

2
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:11,800
He can talk, browse, or leave the question unanswered.

3
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:27,000
He chooses to talk and offers one short line.
This reply is a prepared local example; no model is connected.

4
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:42,000
A possible reading appears. Arjun says “Not quite”
and edits the words himself.

5
00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:50,000
His correction is visible in the conversation.

6
00:00:50,000 --> 00:01:09,000
He confirms that account, then chooses to look at family experiences.
The next step follows his choice, not an automatic model revision.

7
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:23,000
Ravi's fictional account overlaps in one place
and differs in others. Group replies only; no private message option.

8
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:37,000
Arjun reviews the exact audience, alias, and question.
The checkbox is still clear; nothing is sent.

9
00:01:37,000 --> 00:01:44,000
A prepared illustrative sample,
not a live message.

10
00:01:44,000 --> 00:02:01,000
Dev's fictional question leaves room for a bounded story.
Arjun can share what he tried, including what went badly.

11
00:02:01,000 --> 00:02:18,000
He keeps private notes separate, previews the exact group text,
and chooses whether to post this local example.

12
00:02:14,000 --> 00:02:24,000
Professional support is separate.
No provider is verified or bookable here.

13
00:02:24,000 --> 00:02:45,000
Planned Meta Muse Spark — dialogue and structured, correctable context.
Planned Muse Voice Transcribe — optional speech-to-text, reviewed before sending.
Neither is connected in this preview.

14
00:02:46,000 --> 00:02:57,800
He still has questions. One day, if he chooses, he can offer
someone else one honest part of what he tried—and keep the rest private.
